# State Machine Lifecycle

Use a state machine only when an entity has meaningful lifecycle transitions with business rules.

## When To Use

Use state machine services when:

- an entity has multiple statuses;
- not every status can move to every other status;
- transitions have business rules or permissions;
- the UI/API needs available transitions;
- state changes may trigger side effects.

Do not use a state machine for simple active/inactive CRUD or static enums without transition rules.

## Required Structure

- Status enum: stable set of states.
- Transition enum: named actions that cause state changes.
- Transitions definition: valid `from` and `to` pairs.
- State machine service: validates and resolves transitions.
- Tests: valid transitions, invalid transitions, edge cases.

## Service Rules

- Services/use cases load the aggregate, ask the state machine for the transition, then persist.
- State machine code should be deterministic and easy to unit test.
- Side effects belong in application service/use case flow, not hidden inside transition definitions.
- Emit outbox events only when another context needs to react.

## Do Not

- Do not set lifecycle status directly throughout the codebase.
- Do not introduce heavy workflow engines unless business complexity proves the need.
- Do not mix persistence concerns into the state machine service.
